\section{Example Gathering}
\label{sec:example-gathering}

Before invoking the LLM, we need training examples---papers that humans have
already classified with tags. The quality of examples directly impacts
classification accuracy.

\subsection{What Makes a Good Example}

For a paper to serve as an example:
\begin{enumerate}
\item It must be classified (kept or discarded), not pending
\item It must have at least one tag (theme or motivation)
\item A human must have made or confirmed the decision
\item Its category, if it has one, must fit its decision
  (\cref{sec:example-category})
\item Papers where the user corrected an LLM decision are prioritized
  (they represent edge cases the LLM got wrong)
\end{enumerate}

The third rule is the one that keeps the classifier from teaching
itself.  Examples used to be drawn from every decided paper, and sorted
lowest confidence first among the uncorrected ones, so after the first
round the prompt was mostly the model's own least certain unreviewed
guesses, shown to it as the reviewer's judgement.  An error then
propagates: a paper the model wrongly discarded becomes the example of
a discard.  So by default only decisions a human made or confirmed are
examples: source [[human]] or [[llm_reviewed]], and [[agent_reviewed]],
the confirmed form of a decision an AI agent recorded, once that source
exists (issue~\#162).  Unreviewed decisions ([[llm]], and [[agent]])
are left out, and so is any source value this module does not know,
because an unknown source cannot be assumed to be a human's.  The
sources are compared by their stored values rather than by enum
members, so this module keeps working whichever members the review
module defines.  [[include_unreviewed]] (the
[[--include-llm-examples]] option) restores the old behaviour for a
user who wants it; the unreviewed examples then come after the
confirmed ones.

<<llm review constants>>=
CONFIRMED_SOURCES = frozenset({"human", "llm_reviewed", "agent_reviewed"})
UNREVIEWED_MACHINE_SOURCES = frozenset({"llm", "agent"})
<<llm review example gathering>>=
def source_value(decision: ReviewDecision) -> str:
    """Return the stored value of the decision's source (e.g. `"llm"`)."""
    return getattr(decision.source, "value", str(decision.source))


def is_confirmed(decision: ReviewDecision) -> bool:
    """Tell whether a human made or confirmed the decision."""
    return source_value(decision) in CONFIRMED_SOURCES


def is_unreviewed_machine(decision: ReviewDecision) -> bool:
    """Tell whether an LLM or agent decided and no human has reviewed it."""
    return source_value(decision) in UNREVIEWED_MACHINE_SOURCES
@

The first three rules say which decisions are candidates at all; the
fourth then removes some of them, and the fifth only orders what is
left.  The candidates are one function, because the count of records
left out for their category (below) must be taken over exactly the
decisions the examples are drawn from.

<<llm review example gathering>>=
def example_candidates(
    session: ReviewSession, include_unreviewed: bool = False
) -> list[ReviewDecision]:
    """Return the decided, tagged decisions that may serve as examples.

    Those a human made or confirmed, and with `include_unreviewed` also
    unreviewed LLM and agent decisions, in session order.
    """
    return [
        decision for decision in session.decisions
        if decision.tags
        and decision.status in (DecisionStatus.KEPT, DecisionStatus.DISCARDED)
        and (is_confirmed(decision)
             or (include_unreviewed and is_unreviewed_machine(decision)))
    ]
@

\subsection{Examples whose category contradicts their decision}
\label{sec:example-category}

The prompt's category section says that every paper carries exactly
one category and that the category must fit the decision: a keep takes
[[supports-claim]], [[qualifies-claim]] or [[adjacent-subtopic]], a
discard [[adjacent-subtopic]] or [[off-topic-false-hit]]
([[STATUS_CATEGORIES]], \cref{sec:category-fits-status}).  A session
can still hold confirmed decisions that break the rule.  Before
issue~\#177, a status change kept the old category, so a keep under
[[supports-claim]] that a human later discarded stayed tagged
[[supports-claim]]; when the discard corrected the classifier, the
record was also marked [[is_example]], so it came \emph{first} among
the discarded examples (issue~\#255).  A human may also discard a bearing paper on purpose,
under a rule of the review, and the audit table shows that as
\enquote{supports, excluded} (issue~\#89).  Either way, as an example
the record teaches the very pairing the rule forbids, and the parser
then withholds the model's answer when it follows the example.

Such a record is left out of the examples rather than shown without
its category.  Shown without one, it would break the other half of
the rule, exactly one category per paper; and the reason a human
excluded a bearing paper is usually a criterion of the review, which
the prompt tells the model to leave to a human.  The record's
category is the one the audit table reads, its last category tag
([[audit_category]]), so a record whose status change appended a
fitting category is an example again.  A decision without a category
tag cannot contradict, so a session without categories gets the
examples, and so the prompt, it got before.  The check needs nothing
but the decision: a category tag on it is enough to judge it,
whatever vocabulary the run offers.

<<llm review imports>>=
from scholar.review import audit_category
<<llm review example gathering>>=
def category_contradicts_status(decision: ReviewDecision) -> bool:
    """Tell whether the decision's category does not fit its status.

    The category is the last category tag, as in the audit table; a
    decision without one, or a pending one, contradicts nothing.
    """
    category = audit_category(decision.tags)
    status = getattr(decision.status, "value", decision.status)
    return (
        category is not None
        and status in STATUS_CATEGORIES
        and category not in STATUS_CATEGORIES[status]
    )


def contradictory_examples(
    session: ReviewSession, include_unreviewed: bool = False
) -> list[ReviewDecision]:
    """Return the candidate examples left out for their category."""
    return [
        decision
        for decision in example_candidates(session, include_unreviewed)
        if category_contradicts_status(decision)
    ]
@

The records are left out before the lists are sorted and cut to
their maximum, so a record left out gives its place to the next
fitting one instead of shrinking the prompt's examples.  The log that
lists the chosen examples says how many were left out, and
[[llm status]] counts them with the same function, so the two agree.
The prompt version (\cref{sec:prompt-version}) does not move: it
hashes a fixed probe in place of the session's examples, so which
records are examples was never part of it.

<<llm review example gathering>>=
def get_example_decisions(
    session: ReviewSession,
    max_kept: int = MAX_KEPT_EXAMPLES,
    max_discarded: int = MAX_DISCARDED_EXAMPLES,
    include_unreviewed: bool = False,
) -> tuple[list[ReviewDecision], list[ReviewDecision]]:
    """
    Gather tagged examples from a review session.

    Only decisions a human made or confirmed are examples, unless
    `include_unreviewed` is set; then unreviewed machine decisions follow
    the confirmed ones. A decision whose category does not fit its
    status is left out. Prioritizes user-corrected LLM decisions
    (is_example=True) as these represent cases where the LLM's initial
    classification was wrong.

    Args:
        session: The review session to gather examples from
        max_kept: Maximum number of kept examples to include
        max_discarded: Maximum number of discarded examples to include
        include_unreviewed: Also use unreviewed LLM (and agent) decisions

    Returns:
        Tuple of (kept_examples, discarded_examples)
    """
    kept_examples: list[ReviewDecision] = []
    discarded_examples: list[ReviewDecision] = []
    left_out = contradictory_examples(session, include_unreviewed)

    for decision in example_candidates(session, include_unreviewed):
        if category_contradicts_status(decision):
            continue
        if decision.status == DecisionStatus.KEPT:
            kept_examples.append(decision)
        elif decision.status == DecisionStatus.DISCARDED:
            discarded_examples.append(decision)

    # Sort to prioritize corrected examples (is_example=True first),
    # then confirmed before unreviewed, then by confidence (lower first,
    # as these are harder cases)
    def sort_key(d: ReviewDecision) -> tuple[int, int, float]:
        example_priority = 0 if d.is_example else 1
        unreviewed = 0 if is_confirmed(d) else 1
        confidence = d.llm_confidence if d.llm_confidence is not None else 1.0
        return (example_priority, unreviewed, confidence)

    kept_examples.sort(key=sort_key)
    discarded_examples.sort(key=sort_key)

    # Limit to max and log selected examples
    kept_result = kept_examples[:max_kept]
    discarded_result = discarded_examples[:max_discarded]

    # Log info about selected examples
    if kept_result or discarded_result:
        logger.info(
            f"Selected {len(kept_result)} kept and "
            f"{len(discarded_result)} discarded examples for LLM"
        )
        for example in kept_result:
            logger.info(
                f"  KEPT example: {example.paper.title[:60]}... "
                f"[tags: {', '.join(example.tags)}]"
            )
        for example in discarded_result:
            logger.info(
                f"  DISCARDED example: {example.paper.title[:60]}... "
                f"[tags: {', '.join(example.tags)}]"
            )
    if left_out:
        logger.info(
            f"Left out {len(left_out)} examples whose category does not "
            "fit their decision"
        )

    return kept_result, discarded_result
@


\subsection{Computing the Recommended Example Minimum}

A small set of examples gives the LLM enough context to make reasonable
decisions, so we compute a recommended minimum. This check is advisory, not a
gate: [[classify_papers_with_llm]] uses a failed check to warn the user rather
than to block classification, so a review with too few (or no) examples still
runs.

<<llm review example gathering>>=
def validate_examples(
    kept_examples: list[ReviewDecision],
    discarded_examples: list[ReviewDecision],
    min_total: int = MIN_TOTAL_EXAMPLES,
    min_kept: int = MIN_KEPT_EXAMPLES,
    min_discarded: int = MIN_DISCARDED_EXAMPLES,
) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """
    Check if examples meet minimum requirements for LLM classification.

    Args:
        kept_examples: List of kept paper examples
        discarded_examples: List of discarded paper examples
        min_total: Minimum total examples required
        min_kept: Minimum kept examples required
        min_discarded: Minimum discarded examples required

    Returns:
        Tuple of (is_valid, error_message)
        If valid, error_message is empty string.
    """
    total = len(kept_examples) + len(discarded_examples)

    if total < min_total:
        return False, (
            f"Need at least {min_total} tagged examples, "
            f"but only have {total}."
        )

    if len(kept_examples) < min_kept:
        return False, (
            f"Need at least {min_kept} kept example(s) with tags, "
            f"but only have {len(kept_examples)}."
        )

    if len(discarded_examples) < min_discarded:
        return False, (
            f"Need at least {min_discarded} discarded example(s) with tags, "
            f"but only have {len(discarded_examples)}."
        )

    return True, ""
