\section{Prompt Construction}
\label{sec:prompt-construction}

The prompt is critical for effective classification. We structure it to:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Provide research context (what the review is about)
\item Show available tags (themes and motivations already used)
\item Give concrete examples of kept and discarded papers
\item Present papers to classify with their abstracts
\item Request structured JSON output
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Formatting Examples}

We format paper examples to show the key information the LLM should consider.
For the papers being classified, [[_format_paper_to_classify]] can also append
\emph{supporting passages} drawn from the paper's full text.
The abstract alone often cannot settle whether a paper belongs to a theme; a
sentence from the methodology or results can.
When the caller supplies quotes (see \cref{sec:fulltext-classification}), the
classifier therefore assigns labels on evidence the abstract never carried.

Abstracts are cut to bound the prompt, and where they are cut decides
what the model can see.  The limit used to be 1000 characters for every
paper, examples and papers to classify alike.  Measured over the about
114\,000 distinct papers with an abstract in the author's own review
sessions (November 2026; the median abstract is about 1300 characters),
that cut 74\,\% of the abstracts, and three quarters of those by more
than a fifth.  A decision made on part of an abstract is not the
decision the report says was made.  The papers to classify therefore
get [[MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS]] = 4000 characters, which in the same
sessions shows 96\,\% of the abstracts whole.  Some limit is still
needed, because the longest stored \enquote{abstract} there runs to
about 95\,000 characters, and ten such records would fill a prompt on
their own.  When an
abstract is cut, the prompt says so, and the decision records it
([[llm_abstract_truncated]], \cref{sec:llm-audit-fields} in the review
module).  The examples keep the old 1000 characters
([[EXAMPLE_ABSTRACT_CHARS]]): up to twenty of them go into every
prompt, they only illustrate the criteria, and no decision is taken
about them, so raising their limit would let the examples' abstracts
grow to four times their length in every prompt without changing what
any decision rests on.

<<llm review constants>>=
MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS = 4000
EXAMPLE_ABSTRACT_CHARS = 1000
TRUNCATION_MARK = " [abstract truncated]"
<<llm review prompt construction>>=
def abstract_is_truncated(paper: Paper) -> bool:
    """Tell whether the prompt shows only the start of the abstract."""
    return bool(paper.abstract) and len(paper.abstract) > MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS


def _format_paper_for_prompt(
    decision: ReviewDecision,
    include_abstract: bool = True,
) -> str:
    """
    Format a paper decision for inclusion in the prompt.

    Args:
        decision: The review decision containing the paper
        include_abstract: Whether to include the abstract

    Returns:
        Formatted string representation of the paper
    """
    paper = decision.paper
    lines = [
        f"Title: {paper.title}",
        f"Authors: {', '.join(paper.authors[:3])}"
        + (" et al." if len(paper.authors) > 3 else ""),
    ]

    if paper.year:
        lines.append(f"Year: {paper.year}")

    if paper.venue:
        lines.append(f"Venue: {paper.venue}")

    if include_abstract and paper.abstract:
        abstract = paper.abstract
        if len(abstract) > EXAMPLE_ABSTRACT_CHARS:
            abstract = abstract[:EXAMPLE_ABSTRACT_CHARS] + "..."
        lines.append(f"Abstract: {abstract}")

    lines.append(f"Tags: {', '.join(example_tags(decision.tags))}")

    return "\n".join(lines)


def _format_paper_to_classify(
    decision: ReviewDecision,
    index: int,
    quotes: list | None = None,
) -> str:
    """
    Format a paper for classification request.

    Args:
        decision: The review decision containing the paper
        index: Zero-based index for reference in response
        quotes: Optional supporting passages from the paper's full text
            (QuoteCandidate objects with ``page`` and ``text``), shown so the
            LLM can classify on evidence beyond the abstract

    Returns:
        Formatted string representation
    """
    paper = decision.paper
    lines = [
        f"[Paper {index}]",
        f"Title: {paper.title}",
        f"Authors: {', '.join(paper.authors[:3])}"
        + (" et al." if len(paper.authors) > 3 else ""),
    ]

    if paper.year:
        lines.append(f"Year: {paper.year}")

    if paper.venue:
        lines.append(f"Venue: {paper.venue}")

    if paper.abstract:
        abstract = paper.abstract
        if abstract_is_truncated(paper):
            abstract = abstract[:MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS] + TRUNCATION_MARK
        lines.append(f"Abstract: {abstract}")
    else:
        lines.append("Abstract: [Not available]")

    if quotes:
        lines.append("Supporting passages (full text):")
        for quote in quotes:
            lines.append(f'- p.{quote.page}: "{quote.text}"')

    return "\n".join(lines)
@


\subsection{Which tags an example shows}
\label{sec:example-tags}

An example shows its record's tags, so that the model sees which tags
go with which papers.  Note tags are left out, since they are the
reviewer's own (\cref{sec:classifier-categories}).  Of the category
tags only the deciding one is shown, the last, which is the one the
audit table reads and the one by which the example was judged to fit
its decision (\cref{sec:example-category}).  The prompt demands
exactly one category per paper, so an example showing two would
teach what the parser then refuses; a record discarded with
[[supports-claim]] and later [[off-topic-false-hit]] would appear
under the discarded examples as \enquote{supports-claim,
off-topic-false-hit}.  Since issue~\#195, [[sessions decide]] keeps one
category per record ([[tags_after_decision]] in the review module), so
a record with two was written before that, or by a path that adds
tags without that rule.  The earlier category tags are dropped, the
deciding one shown once, at its own place; themes, motivations and
other tags stay in their order.  A record with one category tag or
none therefore shows exactly the tags it showed before.

Only the examples go through this: [[_format_paper_for_prompt]] is
called for the kept and discarded examples alone, and the papers to
classify are pending and show no tags.

<<llm review prompt construction>>=
def example_tags(tags: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Return the tags an example shows in the prompt.

    Note tags are left out, and of the category tags only the deciding
    (last) one is kept; the other tags keep their order.
    """
    deciding = max(
        (i for i, t in enumerate(tags) if t in AUDIT_CATEGORIES),
        default=None,
    )
    return [
        t for i, t in enumerate(tags)
        if t.casefold() not in NOTE_TAGS
        and (t not in AUDIT_CATEGORIES or i == deciding)
    ]
@

