\subsection{Versioning the prompt}
\label{sec:prompt-version}

A decision is only as reproducible as the instructions it was made
under, and those change: this chapter has changed the prompt several
times (the vocabulary rule, the category section, the abstract limit).
Storing the full prompt with every decision would be exact but huge,
and most of it is the batch's own papers.  What a reader needs is
narrower: were two decisions made under the same procedure?  So every
decision of a batch records [[llm_prompt_version]], a hash of the
procedure, and the procedure is the part of the prompt that does not
depend on which papers happen to be in the batch.

The template is captured by building the prompt, and the retry
reminder, for a fixed probe: a placeholder paper with a supporting
passage, one placeholder example of each kind and one placeholder tag
in every list.  Any change to the wording, the section headings or the
way a paper is shown changes the probe's prompt and so the hash,
without anyone having to remember to bump a version number.  The
options are the ones that change what the model is told or shown: the
research context, which the prompt carries verbatim; the tags seeded
with [[--tag]]; whether new tags are allowed; which examples are used;
the full-text passages; the batch size; and the abstract and example
limits.  Left out on purpose are the session's own tags and examples,
which grow every round by design: with them in the hash, every batch
would get a version of its own and the version could no longer group
decisions made the same way.  The model is recorded separately
([[llm_model]]).  Sixteen hexadecimal digits are plenty to tell a
handful of procedures apart and short enough to read in a table.

<<llm review constants>>=
PROMPT_VERSION_DIGITS = 16
<<llm review prompt construction>>=
def classification_prompt_version(
    *,
    research_context: str | None,
    vocabulary: set[str] | None,
    allow_new_tags: bool,
    examples: str,
    use_fulltext: bool,
    max_quotes: int,
    count: int,
) -> str:
    """Return a short hash of the classification procedure.

    `examples` names which examples the prompt uses: `"none"`,
    `"confirmed"` or `"including-unreviewed"`.
    """
    from types import SimpleNamespace

    def probe(title, status=DecisionStatus.PENDING, tags=()):
        return ReviewDecision(
            paper=Paper(title=title, authors=["AUTHOR"], year=2000,
                        venue="VENUE", abstract="ABSTRACT"),
            provider="probe", status=status, tags=list(tags),
        )

    template = build_classification_prompt(
        papers_to_classify=[probe("PAPER")],
        kept_examples=[probe("KEPT", DecisionStatus.KEPT, ["THEME"])],
        discarded_examples=[
            probe("DISCARDED", DecisionStatus.DISCARDED, ["MOTIVATION"])
        ],
        research_context="CONTEXT",
        available_themes={"THEME"},
        available_motivations={"MOTIVATION"},
        paper_quotes=[[SimpleNamespace(page=1, text="PASSAGE")]],
        extra_tags={"TAG"},
        allow_new_tags=allow_new_tags,
        category_tags=set(AUDIT_CATEGORIES),
    )
    procedure = {
        "template": template,
        "retry": _retry_reminder({"TAG"}, set(AUDIT_CATEGORIES)),
        "research_context": research_context or "",
        "vocabulary": sorted(vocabulary or ()),
        "allow_new_tags": allow_new_tags,
        "examples": examples,
        "max_examples": [MAX_KEPT_EXAMPLES, MAX_DISCARDED_EXAMPLES],
        "use_fulltext": use_fulltext,
        "max_quotes": max_quotes if use_fulltext else None,
        "count": count,
        "abstract_chars": [MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS, EXAMPLE_ABSTRACT_CHARS],
    }
    digest = hashlib.sha256(
        json.dumps(procedure, sort_keys=True).encode("utf-8")
    ).hexdigest()
    return digest[:PROMPT_VERSION_DIGITS]
