\subsection{Identifying Papers Needing Enrichment}

Papers without abstracts are poor candidates for LLM classification. We
identify them so they can be enriched before classification.

Since the enrichment functions return new [[Paper]] objects (they do not mutate
the existing ones), we must write the enriched papers back onto the
corresponding [[ReviewDecision]] objects. This ensures the enriched abstracts
are persisted when the session is saved, so later [[scholar sessions resume]]
already has them.

<<llm review llm interaction>>=
def get_papers_needing_enrichment(
    papers: list[ReviewDecision],
) -> list[Paper]:
    """
    Return papers that lack abstracts (required for LLM classification).

    Args:
        papers: List of review decisions to check

    Returns:
        List of Paper objects that need enrichment
    """
    return [
        decision.paper
        for decision in papers
        if not decision.paper.abstract
    ]
@


\subsection{Main Classification Function}

This is the primary entry point for LLM classification. It coordinates
example gathering, prompt construction, LLM invocation, and response parsing.

By default, we ground the LLM with a small set of human-reviewed example
papers, which increases consistency. These examples are a help, not a
precondition: early-stage workflows (e.g., research-question driven searches)
often have nothing tagged yet, and forcing the user to hand-label a quota
before any automation can run defeats the purpose of the automation. So when
the available examples fall below the threshold in [[validate_examples]] we
emit a warning and proceed with whatever examples exist, degrading smoothly
from few-shot to zero-shot rather than refusing to run.

Setting [[require_examples=False]] is the stronger, explicit form: it skips
example gathering entirely for pure \enquote{zero-shot} classification, even
when curated examples are available, relying only on the session's research
context.

<<llm review llm interaction>>=
def classify_papers_with_llm(
    session: ReviewSession,
    count: int = DEFAULT_BATCH_SIZE,
    model_selection=None,
    *,
    model_id: str | None = None,
    enrich_missing: bool = True,
    dry_run: bool = False,
    require_examples: bool = True,
    use_fulltext: bool = False,
    max_quotes: int = 3,
    vocabulary: set[str] | None = None,
    allow_new_tags: bool = False,
    include_llm_examples: bool = False,
    papers: list[ReviewDecision] | None = None,
) -> LLMBatchResult | str:
    """
    Classify pending papers using LLM.

    This is the main entry point for LLM-assisted classification. It:
    1. Optionally gathers training examples from human-reviewed papers
    2. Warns (but does not fail) if examples fall below the recommended
       minimum, then proceeds with whatever examples exist
    3. Optionally enriches papers lacking abstracts
    4. Optionally extracts full-text supporting passages
    5. Constructs a prompt with examples and papers to classify
    6. Invokes the LLM and parses the response

    Args:
        session: The review session
        count: Number of papers to classify in this batch
        model_selection: LLM model selection policy (task override → base → llm default)
        model_id: Deprecated compatibility alias for selecting the analytic model
        vocabulary: Tags the model may use besides the session's existing
            themes and motivations (seeds a fresh session's vocabulary)
        allow_new_tags: Let the model propose tags outside the vocabulary
            instead of dropping them and retrying
        include_llm_examples: Also use unreviewed LLM (and agent)
            decisions as examples, after the confirmed ones
        enrich_missing: Whether to auto-enrich papers without abstracts
        dry_run: If True, return the prompt without calling LLM
        require_examples: If True (default), gather tagged example papers
            and use them to ground the LLM. Insufficient or missing examples
            produce a warning, not an error. Set False to skip example
            gathering entirely for zero-shot classification using only the
            research context.
        use_fulltext: If True, extract supporting passages from each paper's
            full text (matched against the research context) and include them
            in the prompt, so classification can use evidence beyond the
            abstract. Requires the session to have a research context.
        max_quotes: Maximum supporting passages per paper when use_fulltext.
        papers: Classify these decisions (the first `count`) instead of
            the pending ones, as a second screen does; they are left out
            of the examples and of the tag vocabulary, so the prompt
            holds no verdict of theirs. The decisions are not changed.

    Returns:
        LLMBatchResult with decisions, or prompt string if dry_run=True

    Raises:
        ValueError: If there are no pending papers to classify
        ImportError: If llm package is not installed
    """
    <<hide the papers being screened from the examples and vocabulary>>
    # Gather examples (optional)
    if require_examples:
        kept_examples, discarded_examples = get_example_decisions(
            visible, include_unreviewed=include_llm_examples
        )

        # Below-threshold examples are a quality concern, not a hard stop:
        # warn and continue with whatever was gathered (possibly none) so
        # classification still runs early in a review.
        is_valid, error = validate_examples(kept_examples, discarded_examples)
        if not is_valid:
            logger.warning(
                f"{error} Proceeding with the available examples; "
                "classification quality may be reduced."
            )
    else:
        kept_examples = []
        discarded_examples = []

    if papers is not None:
        to_classify = list(papers[:count])
        if not to_classify:
            raise ValueError("No papers to screen")
    else:
        # Get pending papers
        pending = [
            d for d in session.decisions
            if d.status == DecisionStatus.PENDING
        ]

        if not pending:
            raise ValueError("No pending papers to classify")

        # Limit to requested count
        to_classify = pending[:count]

    # Check for papers needing enrichment.
    # Note: enrich_papers() returns new Paper objects, so we must apply the
    # results back onto the decisions to persist enrichment to disk.
    if enrich_missing:
        decisions_to_enrich = [
            d for d in to_classify if not d.paper.abstract
        ]
        if decisions_to_enrich:
            try:
                from scholar.enrich import enrich_papers

                logger.info(
                    f"Enriching {len(decisions_to_enrich)} papers "
                    "before classification"
                )
                enriched = enrich_papers(
                    [d.paper for d in decisions_to_enrich],
                    fields=["abstract"],
                )
                for decision, enriched_paper in zip(
                    decisions_to_enrich, enriched
                ):
                    decision.paper = enriched_paper
            except ImportError:
                logger.warning(
                    "Enrich module not available, "
                    "some papers may lack abstracts"
                )

    # Optionally extract full-text supporting passages for each paper.
    paper_quotes = None
    if use_fulltext and session.research_context:
        paper_quotes = _gather_supporting_quotes(
            to_classify, session.research_context, max_quotes
        )

    <<sort the vocabulary into categories, notes and themes>>

    # Build prompt
    prompt = build_classification_prompt(
        papers_to_classify=to_classify,
        kept_examples=kept_examples,
        discarded_examples=discarded_examples,
        research_context=session.research_context,
        available_themes=themes - set_apart,
        available_motivations=motivations - set_apart,
        paper_quotes=paper_quotes,
        extra_tags=set(vocabulary or ()) - set_apart,
        allow_new_tags=allow_new_tags,
        category_tags=categories,
    )

    if dry_run:
        return prompt

    # Import llm and call
    try:
        import llm as llm_module
    except ImportError:
        raise ImportError(
            "The 'llm' package is required for LLM classification. "
            "Install it with: pip install llm"
        )

    llm = globals().get("llm", llm_module)

    from scholar.utils import select_model_id

    selected_model_id = model_id
    if selected_model_id is None and model_selection is not None:
        selected_model_id = select_model_id(model_selection, "analytic")

    model = (
        llm.get_model(selected_model_id)
        if selected_model_id
        else llm.get_model()
    )
    logger.info(f"Classifying {len(to_classify)} papers with {model.model_id}")

    # Log papers being sent for classification
    logger.info("Papers to classify:")
    for i, decision in enumerate(to_classify):
        abstract_status = "with abstract" if decision.paper.abstract else "NO ABSTRACT"
        logger.info(
            f"  [{i}] {decision.paper.title[:50]}... ({abstract_status})"
        )

    response = model.prompt(prompt)
    response_text = response.text()

    # Parse response, keeping the model inside the vocabulary unless new
    # tags are welcome
    allowed = None if allow_new_tags else (known or None)

    def parse(text: str) -> list[LLMDecision]:
        return parse_llm_response(
            text, to_classify, allowed_tags=allowed,
            excluded_tags=NOTE_TAGS, category_tags=categories,
        )

    decisions = parse(response_text)
    if _needs_retry(decisions, allowed):
        logger.warning(
            "Some papers were left without tags, without exactly one "
            "category or with a category that does not fit the decision; "
            "retrying once with the vocabulary spelled out"
        )
        response = model.prompt(
            prompt + _retry_reminder(allowed, categories)
        )
        response_text = response.text()
        decisions = parse(response_text)

    <<withhold the decisions that cannot be applied>>

    return LLMBatchResult(
        decisions=decisions,
        model_id=model.model_id,
        timestamp=datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
        prompt_tokens=getattr(response, "prompt_tokens", None),
        completion_tokens=getattr(response, "completion_tokens", None),
        withheld=withheld,
        passages={
            decision.paper.id: [quote.text for quote in quotes]
            for decision, quotes in zip(to_classify, paper_quotes or [])
            if quotes
        },
        prompt_version=classification_prompt_version(
            research_context=session.research_context,
            vocabulary=vocabulary,
            allow_new_tags=allow_new_tags,
            examples=(
                "none" if not require_examples
                else "including-unreviewed" if include_llm_examples
                else "confirmed"
            ),
            use_fulltext=bool(use_fulltext and session.research_context),
            max_quotes=max_quotes,
            count=count,
        ),
    )
@

The batch's time is taken in UTC when the answer is in, because it is
the time stored with every decision of the batch
([[llm_decided_at]]); the local time it used to be, without a zone,
would have been ambiguous as soon as a session was classified on two
machines.

The batch also hands back the full-text passages the model was shown,
by paper id ([[passages]]).  The model is asked for a reason, and a
quote in it may come from such a passage rather than from the abstract;
the passages are not stored with the decision, so only the run that
showed them can check a quote against them (the quote check of the
review module, \cref{sec:reason-quotes} there, which
[[llm classify]] runs at the end, \cref{sec:llm-classify-quotes} in
the CLI module).

The same function classifies the papers of a second screen
(\cref{sec:second-screen}), given as [[papers]].  Those papers are
decided, and the prompt must hold no verdict of theirs.  The papers to
classify never showed one (title, authors, year, venue, abstract and
passages only), but the session's other parts would: a screened paper
that a human confirmed is a candidate example, shown with its label,
so the model would be handed the answer about the very paper it is
asked to judge; and its tags join the vocabulary.  So the examples and
the vocabulary are taken from [[visible]], a shallow copy of the
session without the papers being classified and without every other
record of the same works (below), and everything else (enrichment,
passages, the answer's checks and retry) runs as for the pending
papers.  Taking them out of the session before the examples
are chosen, rather than out of the chosen examples, lets the next
fitting example take a screened paper's place instead of shrinking
the prompt.  An ordinary run passes no [[papers]], and [[visible]] is
the session itself.

