\subsection{Keeping the classifier inside the vocabulary}

A session's existing themes and motivations are the vocabulary its
tables and appendices are built on, and a tag the model invents
(\enquote{Machine Learning} beside an existing \enquote{ml}) silently
falls outside every count. The prompt already lists the vocabulary;
now it also says to use nothing else, [[parse_llm_response]] maps each
returned tag onto the vocabulary case-insensitively and drops what does
not match (recording it in [[dropped_tags]] and warning), and when a
paper would be left without any tag the batch is sent once more with
the vocabulary spelled out, since a second attempt is cheaper than a
paper that has to be tagged by hand. A session without tags yet
imposes nothing, so the first round is free to propose themes---unless
the caller seeds the vocabulary: [[vocabulary]] (the [[--tag]] option)
adds tags that count as known from the first batch on, which is how a
two-sided claim review constrains a fresh session to its four
categories (\cref{sec:classifier-categories}). The opposite is
sometimes wanted too: with [[allow_new_tags]] (the
[[--allow-new-tags]] option) the prompt asks the model to prefer the
listed tags but to add one when none fits, and no tag is dropped for
being new, so an exploratory first pass can grow the vocabulary; only
the rules for note and category tags in the next subsection still
apply.

