\subsection{Category, theme and note tags}
\label{sec:classifier-categories}

The vocabulary check above makes sure every tag the model returns is
one the session knows, but not that it is the right \emph{kind} of tag.
A two-sided claim review uses three kinds side by side: exactly one
audit \emph{category} per paper (supports the claim, qualifies it,
adjacent subtopic, false hit), optional \emph{theme} tags on the papers
that get cited, and \emph{note} tags such as
\enquote{duplicate-record-of-cited-source} that a human sets on a record
deliberately not cited (see the audit table in the review module).
Offered all of them as one flat list, a model returned exactly the
mixtures one would fear (issue~\#91): rows with only a theme and no
category, rows with two categories, and note tags copied onto unrelated
rows. Every one of those rows passes the vocabulary check and is then
misfiled by the audit table, and [[--bearing-only]] silently hides the
ones without a category.

The fix keeps each kind in its place. Note tags record a judgement
about a \emph{record}---this is a reprint, that one a duplicate---which
the model cannot make from an abstract, so they are human annotations:
they are never offered (not in the lists and not on the examples' tag
lines, where the model would learn to copy them) and are dropped like
any out-of-vocabulary tag if the model returns one anyway, whatever the
language of the table they are named for. The audit categories, when
the session uses any, are presented apart as a choice of exactly one,
with the theme lists as optional extras. The categories are the audit
table's own [[AUDIT_CATEGORIES]], so the classifier and the table cannot
drift apart, and once the session uses any of them, all four are
offered, whichever the session's records happen to carry so far.  The
categories are one scale, not a vocabulary a review grows: a session
whose records carry only [[qualifies-claim]] has no category for a
discard, and one with only [[adjacent-subtopic]] and
[[off-topic-false-hit]] none for a paper that supports the claim.
Offering only those made the model either break the rule that the
category fits the decision (\cref{sec:category-fits-status}), so the
paper was withheld after a wasted retry, or file a supporting paper
under \enquote{adjacent}.  The categories added this way join the
vocabulary the answers are checked against, so the parser keeps them;
the themes and motivations stay the session's own, and the examples
show the tags their records carry.  A session whose vocabulary holds
no category tag is classified as before.

The themes and motivations are read from [[visible]], which is the
session itself except in a second screen (\cref{sec:second-screen}):
there it lacks the decided papers being screened, because a tag only
such a paper carries would tell the model how that paper was judged
(a motivation such as \enquote{simulation-only} exists because some
paper was discarded for it).  Whether the session uses categories is
still read from the whole session: the answer offers all four or none,
whichever paper carries one, so it says nothing about any paper's
verdict, and a second screen over every decided paper would otherwise
lose the categories its comparison with the first screen needs.

