\subsection{A category that fits the decision}
\label{sec:category-fits-status}

The one-category rule says nothing about \emph{which} category goes
with which decision, and the model used that freedom: in the sessions
at hand, two dozen unreviewed classifier discards carry
[[supports-claim]] or [[qualifies-claim]], and one keep carries
[[off-topic-false-hit]]
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/224}).  The audit
table shows such a discard as \enquote{supports the claim, excluded}:
the status wins over the category (issue~\#89, see the audit table in
the review module), so the row is never counted as evidence, but it
records two opposite verdicts of one model in one decision, and nobody
has looked at it.

The state itself is not an error.  The review module renders it on
purpose, as \enquote{\emph{category}, excluded}, and counts it apart
in the caption (\enquote{excluded although classified as bearing on
the claim}), because a reviewer may exclude a hit that bears on the
claim under a rule of the review---a venue, a method, a date---and
[[sessions decide]] records such a decision as given.  What is wrong
is a \emph{machine} making it.  Excluding a relevant hit is a
judgement about the review's own rules, and the model can only guess
at those; a discard it makes under a bearing category is exactly the
row a human has to look at, and recording it as a decision hides it
among the ones nobody needs to.  So only the classifier's answers are
checked, at parse time, and a human's decision is left as it is.

The categories each decision admits follow from what they say.
[[supports-claim]] and [[qualifies-claim]] bear on the claim, so they
go with a keep; a discard under them is the contradiction above.
[[off-topic-false-hit]] says the search matched a paper that is not
about the subject, so it goes with a discard: a kept false hit is a
paper the model says is not about the question and still puts forward.
[[adjacent-subtopic]] fits both: an adjacent paper is discarded as a
rule, but kept for background as well, and the audit table reads it
the same either way, as not bearing on the claim.

