Telling the model only after the fact would waste the first answer, so
the rule goes into the category section of the prompt and into the
retry's reminder, with the categories each decision admits.  Since
the classifier offers all four categories whenever it offers any
(\cref{sec:classifier-categories}), the rule names the full sets, and
every decision has a category to take.  The rule also says what to do with a paper that bears
on the claim but seems excludable: keep it.  Without that, a model
told only that a discard needs another category could relabel a
supporting paper as adjacent to discard it, which would be a worse
answer than the contradiction: one that looks consistent and is not.
The sentence is fixed whatever the categories, so the prompt version
(\cref{sec:prompt-version}) moves with the rule; its probe offers the
four categories, as the classifier does.

<<llm review llm interaction>>=
def _category_status_rule(categories: set[str] | None) -> str:
    """Return the prompt's rule of which category fits which decision."""
    if not categories:
        return ""

    def fitting(status: str) -> str:
        return ", ".join(sorted(
            STATUS_CATEGORIES[status], key=AUDIT_CATEGORIES.get
        ))

    return (
        "The category must fit the decision: a kept paper takes "
        f"{fitting('kept')}; a discarded paper takes "
        f"{fitting('discarded')}. Keep a paper that bears on the claim "
        "even if something else counts against it: whether to exclude "
        "it is for a human to decide."
    )
