\subsection{Gathering Full-text Support}
\label{sec:fulltext-classification}

When [[use_fulltext]] is set, we look for passages in each paper's full text
that support the research context, and feed them to the classifier alongside
the abstract.
This is the same quote-extraction machinery the [[scholar pdf quote]] command
uses, reused as a library (\cref{sec:quote-extraction}).

We run the \emph{lexical} ranker only, not the LLM re-ranker.
The classifier already spends one LLM call per batch to assign labels; ordering
each paper's passages with a second LLM call would multiply cost without adding
much, since the classification call itself judges relevance.
The cheap lexical prefilter simply surfaces the candidate sentences worth
showing it.

Quote extraction is best-effort per paper: a paper with no open-access PDF
yields no quotes and falls back to abstract-only classification.
[[find_quote_candidates]] already returns an empty list on download or parse
failure, but we still guard each call so one unexpected error cannot abort the
whole batch.

<<llm review llm interaction>>=
def _gather_supporting_quotes(
    papers: list[ReviewDecision],
    claim: str,
    max_quotes: int,
) -> list[list]:
    """Find supporting passages for each paper, aligned by index.

    Args:
        papers: The review decisions being classified.
        claim: The research context to match passages against.
        max_quotes: Maximum passages to keep per paper.

    Returns:
        A list parallel to ``papers``; entry ``i`` holds the supporting
        passages for paper ``i`` (empty when none were found).
    """
    from scholar.pdf import find_quote_candidates

    quotes_by_paper: list[list] = []
    for decision in papers:
        try:
            quotes = find_quote_candidates(
                decision.paper,
                claim,
                limit=max_quotes,
                use_lexical=True,
                use_llm=False,
            )
        except Exception as error:  # one bad paper must not abort the batch
            logger.warning(
                "Quote extraction failed for '%s': %s",
                decision.paper.title[:50],
                error,
            )
            quotes = []
        quotes_by_paper.append(quotes)
    return quotes_by_paper
