\section{Sampling machine decisions for review}
\label{sec:llm-sample}

A confidence score is the model's own report of its certainty, and a
review that leaves most LLM decisions unreviewed has, so far, no
measure of how often those decisions are wrong.  In the author's
sessions about 85\,000 LLM discards were unreviewed in November 2026
(78\,000 distinct papers); reviewing
them all would defeat the purpose of the classifier, but reviewing a
\emph{random sample} of them gives an estimate, with an interval, of
the rate at which a human would overturn them.  With it, the report
can say about the automated screening step not only that it ran, but
how well it agreed with a human on a sample the human did not choose.

This section has three parts.  [[draw_llm_sample]] draws a reproducible
sample and records it in the sessions; [[sample_reports]] counts, once
the sample has been reviewed, how many decisions the reviewer changed
and puts a modified Wilson interval around the rate; and
[[build_llm_screening_latex]] turns both, and the classifier's record
of its decisions, into the paragraphs of the review report.  The
command [[scholar llm sample]] in the CLI drives the first two; the TUI
queues the sample for review (filter [[s]]); and [[sessions decide]]
reviews a sampled paper like any other.

\subsection{The population and the draw}

The population is every \emph{unreviewed machine decision} of the
pooled sessions that matches the criteria: its source is [[llm]] (or
[[agent]], the unreviewed decision of an AI agent, once the review
module has that source), its status is one of the requested ones
(discarded, kept or both), and, with [[min_confidence]], its confidence
is at least that.  A decision without a confidence is left out when a
minimum is asked for, since it cannot be shown to meet it.  Each
decision appears once, keyed by its session's name and its paper id.

A review that finds the classifier wrong mostly at low confidence wants
to sample exactly there, so a population can also be bounded from
above (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/254}).  The
upper bound [[max_confidence]] is exclusive: a confidence must be
\emph{below} it.  With an inclusive lower bound and an exclusive upper
one, the populations [[0.5 <= c < 0.7]] and [[0.7 <= c]] meet without
overlapping, so two draws with adjacent bounds never draw the same
decision.  A decision without a confidence fails either bound, as it
fails the minimum.  Those decisions can still be selected on purpose,
and only them, with [[no_confidence]]; that criterion cannot be
combined with a bound, since together they would select nothing.

<<llm review sampling>>=
def confidence_in_bounds(
    confidence: float | None,
    min_confidence: float | None,
    max_confidence: float | None,
) -> bool:
    """Tell whether `confidence` lies in `[min_confidence, max_confidence)`.

    A missing bound is no constraint; a missing confidence meets no bound.
    """
    if min_confidence is None and max_confidence is None:
        return True
    if confidence is None:
        return False
    return (
        (min_confidence is None or confidence >= min_confidence)
        and (max_confidence is None or confidence < max_confidence)
    )
@

The draw has to be reproducible from what is stored: given the seed
and the population, anyone must get the same papers.  Python's
[[random.sample]] is not enough for that, because the documentation of
the [[random]] module promises only that [[random()]] keeps producing
the same sequence for a given seed across Python versions, and says
that most of the module's other algorithms may change (\enquote{Notes
on Reproducibility},
\url{https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html}, read 2026-11-18).
So the population is sorted by session name and paper id, each member
gets the next value of [[random()]] as its key, in that order, and the
sample is the [[size]] members with the smallest keys.  Every member
gets an independent uniform key, so every ordering of the keys is
equally likely, and so is every set of [[size]] members: it is a simple
random sample without replacement.  Ties, which [[random()]] makes
practically impossible, fall back on the sorted order.

The sample is recorded in every pooled session (\cref{sec:llm-sample-record}
in the review module) with the criteria, the population's size and a
digest of its sorted keys, so that a reader can check later that the
population a re-draw starts from is the one the sample was drawn from.
The criteria also list which machine sources the population held, so
that the report can say \enquote{LLM decisions} when no agent decision
was among them.

<<llm review sampling>>=
@dataclass
class SampleDraw:
    """The outcome of `draw_llm_sample`: the id and the drawn decisions.

    `bands` has one entry per stratum of a stratified draw, as
    `{"band", "population", "drawn", "census"}`, and is empty otherwise.
    """
    sample_id: str
    population: int
    drawn: list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision]]
    bands: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)


def session_label(session: ReviewSession) -> str:
    """Return the name a session is known by in a sample."""
    return session.name or session.query


def sample_population(
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
    *,
    statuses: set[str],
    min_confidence: float | None = None,
    max_confidence: float | None = None,
    no_confidence: bool = False,
) -> list[tuple[str, str, ReviewDecision]]:
    """Return the eligible decisions as sorted `(session, paper id, decision)`.

    Eligible are unreviewed LLM or agent decisions whose status value is in
    `statuses` and whose confidence lies in `[min_confidence,
    max_confidence)`, or, with `no_confidence`, that have no confidence.
    """
    from scholar.notes import get_paper_id

    pool: dict[tuple[str, str], ReviewDecision] = {}
    for session in sessions:
        name = session_label(session)
        for decision in session.decisions:
            if not is_unreviewed_machine(decision):
                continue
            if decision.status.value not in statuses:
                continue
            if no_confidence:
                if decision.llm_confidence is not None:
                    continue
            elif not confidence_in_bounds(
                decision.llm_confidence, min_confidence, max_confidence
            ):
                continue
            pool.setdefault((name, get_paper_id(decision.paper)), decision)
    return [(name, pid, d) for (name, pid), d in sorted(pool.items())]
@

The draw is one procedure for three kinds of sample.  A plain sample
has one population, one stratum, drawn as described above.  A
stratified sample (\cref{sec:llm-sample-bands}) has one stratum per
confidence band, each drawn the same way with a random generator of its
own.  A census takes every member of every stratum, and so needs
neither a size nor a seed.  Each stratum is kept with its population,
its generator and, after the draw, its drawn members, so that the
recording step can store each one with its own population and digest.

<<llm review sampling>>=
@dataclass
class SampleStratum:
    """One stratum of a draw: its band (None for a plain sample) and pool."""
    band: "ConfidenceBand | None"
    pool: list[tuple[str, str, ReviewDecision]]
    rng: random.Random | None
    drawn: list[tuple[str, str, ReviewDecision]] = field(default_factory=list)
    census: bool = False


def draw_llm_sample(
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
    *,
    statuses: set[str],
    seed: int | None,
    size: int | None = None,
    min_confidence: float | None = None,
    max_confidence: float | None = None,
    no_confidence: bool = False,
    bands: list[float] | None = None,
    census: bool = False,
    now: datetime | None = None,
) -> SampleDraw:
    """Draw a seeded random sample of unreviewed machine decisions.

    Records the sample in every session of `sessions` (as an `LLMSample`
    in `session.llm_samples`); the caller saves them. With `bands`, cut
    points strictly increasing in (0, 1), the draw is stratified: `size`
    decisions from each confidence band (all of a band with no more),
    each band recorded as its own `LLMSample` under one group id. With
    `census`, every eligible decision is taken, and no seed is needed.

    Raises:
        ValueError: `size` or `seed` is missing without `census`, the
            criteria contradict each other, or no decision is eligible.
    """
    from scholar.review import LLMSample, session_file_path

    <<check the criteria of the draw>>
    if bands is None:
        strata = [SampleStratum(
            band=None,
            pool=sample_population(
                sessions, statuses=statuses, min_confidence=min_confidence,
                max_confidence=max_confidence, no_confidence=no_confidence,
            ),
            rng=random.Random(seed) if seed is not None else None,
        )]
    else:
        strata = band_strata(sessions, statuses=statuses, cuts=bands, seed=seed)
    if not any(stratum.pool for stratum in strata):
        raise ValueError("No unreviewed LLM decisions match the criteria")
    <<draw the members with the smallest keys>>
    <<record the sample in each session>>
    return SampleDraw(
        sample_id=sample_id,
        population=sum(len(stratum.pool) for stratum in strata),
        drawn=[
            (name, decision)
            for stratum in strata for name, _, decision in stratum.drawn
        ],
        bands=[
            {"band": stratum.band.label, "population": len(stratum.pool),
             "drawn": len(stratum.drawn), "census": stratum.census}
            for stratum in strata if stratum.band is not None
        ],
    )
@

The criteria are checked before anything is read, so that a draw that
cannot mean anything fails as a whole.  The command line checks the
same rules and turns them into usage errors; they are repeated here
because the function is also called directly.

<<check the criteria of the draw>>=
if bands is not None:
    check_band_cuts(bands)
    if min_confidence is not None or max_confidence is not None \
            or no_confidence:
        raise ValueError(
            "Confidence bands cannot be combined with confidence bounds")
if no_confidence and (min_confidence is not None
                      or max_confidence is not None):
    raise ValueError(
        "Decisions without a confidence cannot be bounded by one")
if not census and (size is None or size < 1):
    raise ValueError("The sample size must be at least 1")
if not census and seed is None:
    raise ValueError("A random sample needs a seed")
