\subsection{Confidence bands: one stratified draw}
\label{sec:llm-sample-bands}

A review that checked its screens found the classifier's disagreements
with a human at low and middle confidence, and none among its
high-confidence discards (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/254}).
One simple random sample over all discards spends most of its draws
where the discards are many, which need not be where the errors are.
So the population can be split into confidence bands and a sample of
the same size drawn from each: [[bands]] gives the cut points, say
[[0.5, 0.7, 0.9]], and the bands are \emph{below 0.5}, \emph{0.5 to
below 0.7}, \emph{0.7 to below 0.9} and \emph{at least 0.9}, with the
same inclusive lower and exclusive upper bounds as a single draw.  The
first and the last band are open at their outer end, so every decision
with a confidence is in exactly one band; decisions without one form a
band of their own when there are any.  The bands are therefore
disjoint and together are the whole population, which is what lets the
report weight each band's rate by its size
(\cref{sec:llm-sample-stratified-report}).  The cut points must be
strictly increasing and lie strictly between 0 and 1, else a band would
be empty by construction, and bands cannot be combined with bounds or
with [[no_confidence]]: a band structure over a bounded population
would leave bands that the bound empties.

<<llm review sampling>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ConfidenceBand:
    """A confidence band `[min_confidence, max_confidence)`, or no confidence.

    A missing bound leaves the band open at that end.
    """
    min_confidence: float | None
    max_confidence: float | None
    no_confidence: bool = False

    @property
    def label(self) -> str:
        """Describe the band in words, e.g. '0.5 to below 0.7'."""
        return confidence_criteria_text(
            self.min_confidence, self.max_confidence, self.no_confidence
        )


def confidence_criteria_text(
    min_confidence: float | None,
    max_confidence: float | None,
    no_confidence: bool = False,
) -> str:
    """Describe confidence bounds in words ('' when there are none)."""
    if no_confidence:
        return "without a confidence"
    if min_confidence is not None and max_confidence is not None:
        return f"{min_confidence:g} to below {max_confidence:g}"
    if min_confidence is not None:
        return f"at least {min_confidence:g}"
    if max_confidence is not None:
        return f"below {max_confidence:g}"
    return ""


def check_band_cuts(cuts: list[float]) -> None:
    """Raise `ValueError` unless `cuts` increase strictly within (0, 1)."""
    if not cuts:
        raise ValueError("Give at least one confidence cut point")
    if any(not 0 < cut < 1 for cut in cuts):
        raise ValueError("Confidence cut points must lie between 0 and 1")
    if any(a >= b for a, b in zip(cuts, cuts[1:])):
        raise ValueError("Confidence cut points must increase strictly")


def confidence_bands(cuts: list[float]) -> list[ConfidenceBand]:
    """Return the bands the cut points make, lowest first."""
    edges = [None, *cuts, None]
    return [ConfidenceBand(low, high) for low, high in zip(edges, edges[1:])]
@

A draw in one band must not depend on what the other bands hold.  If
the bands shared one generator, as a single draw does, adding one
decision to the lowest band would consume one more random number before
the next band's turn and shift every key after it: a re-draw with the
same seed would give other papers in every band, although only one band
changed.  So each band gets a generator of its own, seeded from the
sample's seed and the band's own bounds, and draws keys only for its own
members.  Its draw is then a function of its own population and the
seed alone, and each band is a simple random sample of its band by the
argument for a single draw.  The band's seed is the first eight bytes
of a SHA-256 digest of the text [[seed/min/max/no_confidence]] (the
bounds written with Python's [[repr]]), read as an integer, so that the
generator is seeded with an integer as a single draw's is and the
derivation depends on nothing but the stored seed and bounds.  Keying
on the bounds rather than the band's position means that a band keeps
its draw also when other cut points are added around it.

<<llm review sampling>>=
def band_random(seed: int | None, band: ConfidenceBand) -> random.Random:
    """Return the random generator of one band of a stratified draw."""
    text = (f"{seed}/{band.min_confidence!r}/{band.max_confidence!r}/"
            f"{band.no_confidence}")
    digest = hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).digest()
    return random.Random(int.from_bytes(digest[:8], "big"))


def band_strata(
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
    *,
    statuses: set[str],
    cuts: list[float],
    seed: int | None,
) -> list[SampleStratum]:
    """Return one stratum per confidence band, plus one without confidence."""
    bands = confidence_bands(cuts)
    unknown = ConfidenceBand(None, None, no_confidence=True)
    strata = [
        SampleStratum(
            band=band,
            pool=sample_population(
                sessions, statuses=statuses,
                min_confidence=band.min_confidence,
                max_confidence=band.max_confidence,
                no_confidence=band.no_confidence,
            ),
            rng=band_random(seed, band),
        )
        for band in [*bands, unknown]
    ]
    if not strata[-1].pool:
        strata.pop()
    return strata
@

\paragraph{A census.}
A band small enough to review whole should be reviewed whole: a sample
of ten from twelve leaves two unknown for no gain.  So a band with at
most [[size]] members is taken whole, and recorded as a census of that
band.  [[census]] does the same for every band, or for the whole
population of a single draw: all decisions that match the criteria are
taken and recorded like a sample, so that the report covers them.  A
census draws nothing at random and so needs no seed; one given is
recorded, one not given is stored as [[None]].  An older Scholar
(\texttt{4ba7a23}, whose [[sample_sentence]] and [[sample_reports]]
were read for this) knows no census: it states one as a random sample
\enquote{(seed None)} and gives it a modified Wilson interval.

\paragraph{How a stratified sample is recorded.}
A stratified sample has to be recognisable as one population split
into disjoint bands, with each band's bounds, population size and
digest, and each sampled paper's band.  It is stored as one
[[LLMSample]] per band, which share a group id, rather than as one
[[LLMSample]] with a list of bands.  A band needs exactly what a sample
has: its own population, digest, size and drawn papers, and its own
count of papers not found in the sessions reported on, which
[[sample_reports]] computes from the size.  One record per band reuses
all of that, so [[sample_reports]] reports each band as it reports a
sample, and the papers' band is the record that holds them.  The band
records carry the same session files, so the other pooled sessions are
read as for any sample ([[pooled_sample_sessions]]), and each has its
own id, so the concurrent merge, which keeps one sample per id, keeps
every band.  The group, the band's index, the number of bands and the
cut points are kept in the criteria under [[stratum]], and a band's id
is the group id followed by [[/band=]] and its index.

The deciding reason is the older Scholar installations.  They read the
criteria as a whole dictionary and the drawn papers as dictionaries,
and write both back unchanged ([[LLMSample.from_dict]] and
[[to_dict]], \cref{sec:llm-sample-record} in the review module, read at
\texttt{4ba7a23}), so a save by an older version keeps the group.
Such a version reports each band as a sample of its own: its numbers
are right for that band, only its sentence leaves out the upper bound
and the missing confidence, which it does not know.  A single record
with a list of bands would be read by such a version as one random
sample of the whole population, and its rate added up over the bands
without weights, which is not an estimate of anything.

The banded draw is pinned like the single one.  Two sessions with
twenty decisions are split by [[0.5, 0.7, 0.9]] into bands of 7, 3, 5
and 2 decisions, with 3 decisions without a confidence; with two
decisions per band, the band of 2 is a census.  The expected papers
were computed separately, with Python's [[random]] and [[hashlib]]
modules alone, from the rule above (each band sorted by session and
paper id, the band's generator from the digest of
[[42/None/0.5/False]] and so on, the two smallest keys), without this
module.  The boundary values 0.5, 0.7 and 0.9 land in the band they
open.

