\subsection{Reporting a sample}

A sample drawn over several sessions is stored in each of them, so the
report groups the stored samples by id and adds up what it finds in
the sessions it was given; a sample stored twice in one session is
counted once there.  A session that the sample names but that
was not given, or a sampled paper no longer in its session (re-keyed
by a later DOI, say), is not counted, and the report says how many
such papers there were instead of treating them as unreviewed or as
agreed.  The papers not yet reviewed are listed with the session
argument that opens their session ([[session_argument]],
\cref{sec:session-arguments} in the review module), which the user
passes to [[sessions decide]] next: the session's label, or its file
name when the label would open another session or fit two
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/237}).

A census (\cref{sec:llm-sample-bands}) is reported like a sample, but
without an interval: once every member is reviewed, its rate is not an
estimate but the rate of the population it was drawn from, at the time
of the draw.  While part of it is unreviewed, the reviewed part is not
a random sample of it unless the order of review was, and the sentence
says how many are left instead.

A sampled paper can also have been set back to pending after the draw
([[sessions reset]], \cref{sec:reset-decision} in the review module).
The decision the sample drew is then gone: it lives on only in the
paper's reset history, and whatever the paper is decided next is a new
call the sample never drew.  Counted as usual, a reset discard that the
classifier then keeps and a human confirms would read as a discard the
reviewer changed, and the paper would be listed as unreviewed while it
waits for its new call.  So such a paper is counted apart, as
[[reset_since_draw]], never as reviewed, changed or unreviewed
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/248}).  The test is
the paper's reset records: one made at or after the sample's
[[drawn_at]] means the drawn decision was reset.  Both times are UTC
times in the same ISO form, written by [[draw_llm_sample]] and by the
reset from [[datetime.now(timezone.utc)]] with seconds; a reset in the
very second of the draw counts as after it, since a decision reset
just before a draw in the same second would have been pending and so
not drawn.  They are compared as times when both parse, so a time
written with another offset still compares rightly, and as strings
otherwise.  A reset made before the draw is no concern: the sample drew
the decision made after it.

<<llm review sampling>>=
def reset_since_draw(decision: ReviewDecision, drawn_at: str) -> bool:
    """Tell whether `decision` was reset to pending at or after `drawn_at`."""
    for record in getattr(decision, "resets", None) or []:
        at = str(record.get("at") or "")
        try:
            after = (datetime.fromisoformat(at)
                     >= datetime.fromisoformat(drawn_at))
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            after = at >= drawn_at
        if after:
            return True
    return False


@dataclass
class SampleReport:
    """What a sample shows so far, over the sessions it was reported on.

    `changed` counts reviewed decisions whose status differs from the
    machine's; `interval` is the 95% modified Wilson interval of
    `changed` out of `reviewed`, or `None` while nothing is reviewed and
    for a census, whose rate is that of its whole population.
    `unreviewed` holds `(session argument, paper id)` pairs.
    """
    sample_id: str
    seed: int | None
    drawn_at: str
    criteria: dict
    sessions: list[str]
    population: int
    size: int
    reviewed: int = 0
    changed: int = 0
    not_found: int = 0
    reset_since_draw: int = 0
    unreviewed: list[tuple[str, str]] = field(default_factory=list)
    interval: tuple[float, float] | None = None

    @property
    def rate(self) -> float | None:
        """Return the share of reviewed decisions the reviewer changed."""
        return self.changed / self.reviewed if self.reviewed else None

    @property
    def census(self) -> bool:
        """Tell whether the sample took its whole population."""
        return bool(self.criteria.get("census"))


def sample_reports(sessions: list[ReviewSession]) -> list[SampleReport]:
    """Report every sample stored in `sessions`, oldest first."""
    from scholar.notes import get_paper_id

    from scholar.review import session_argument

    reports: dict[str, SampleReport] = {}
    for session in sessions:
        name = session_argument(session)
        current = {get_paper_id(d.paper): d for d in session.decisions}
        counted_here: set[str] = set()
        for sample in session.llm_samples:
            if sample.sample_id in counted_here:
                continue
            counted_here.add(sample.sample_id)
            report = reports.setdefault(sample.sample_id, SampleReport(
                sample_id=sample.sample_id, seed=sample.seed,
                drawn_at=sample.drawn_at, criteria=dict(sample.criteria),
                sessions=list(sample.sessions),
                population=sample.population, size=sample.size,
            ))
            for entry in sample.papers:
                <<count one sampled decision>>
    for report in reports.values():
        <<count the papers of sessions not given>>
        if report.reviewed and not report.census:
            report.interval = modified_wilson_interval(
                report.changed, report.reviewed
            )
    return sorted(reports.values(), key=lambda r: r.drawn_at)
