\subsection{Reporting a stratified sample}
\label{sec:llm-sample-stratified-report}

[[sample_reports]] reports each band of a stratified sample as the
sample it is stored as, with its rate and its modified Wilson interval,
computed as for a single sample: the band was drawn from its own
population in the same way a single sample is.  The report then puts the
bands of one draw back together, by the group recorded in their
criteria, and states them as one sample with one line per band.

What the user wants from the bands together is an estimate for the
whole population: how many of all the machine's discards a reviewer
would change, and how many of those are among the decisions nobody
reviewed.  The rates of the bands cannot simply be added up over the
reviewed decisions: each band drew the same number from populations of
very different sizes, so a decision of a small band stands for few
decisions and one of a large band for many.  Each band's rate has to be
weighted by its band's size.

The estimator is the survey-weighted estimator of a total under
stratified sampling, as Franco, Little, Louis and Slud write it
(\enquote{Comparative Study of Confidence Intervals for Proportions in
Complex Sample Surveys}, \emph{Journal of Survey Statistics and
Methodology} 7(3):334--364, 2019,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1093/jssam/smy019}; read in full in the
authors' final version of 2 August 2018,
\url{https://math.umd.edu/~slud/s770/SurveyConfidenceIntervals/JSSAM-2017-065-FINAL.pdf},
read 2026-11-20, whose pages are cited here).  For a sample of
clusters drawn by simple random sampling within each stratum $h$, they
estimate the population total $Y$ by
\[
  \hat Y = \sum_{h=1}^{H} \hat Y_{h++}, \qquad
  \hat Y_{h++} = \frac{K_h}{n^C_h} \sum_{k \in S_h} Y_{hk+}
\]
(their equation~(10), p.~10), where $K_h$ is the number of clusters in
stratum~$h$, $n^C_h$ the number sampled and $Y_{hk+}$ the count of
successes in cluster~$k$; they call it \enquote{the Horvitz-Thompson
estimator under stratified one stage sampling of clusters} (p.~29).
Here every decision is a cluster of its own, the strata are the bands,
and a success is a decision the reviewer changed, so with $N_h$
decisions in band~$h$, $n_h$ of them reviewed and $x_h$ changed, the
band's term is $N_h x_h / n_h$.  The estimated share of changed
decisions is that total over the population size $N = \sum_h N_h$,
\[
  \hat p = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{h} N_h \frac{x_h}{n_h},
\]
the rate of each band weighted by its share of the population.  The
changed decisions among those reviewed are known, $\sum_h x_h$, so the
estimated number among the decisions not reviewed is the estimated
total less that: $\sum_h (N_h - n_h)\, x_h / n_h$.  A census band
contributes its known count and nothing to estimate.

