\paragraph{When the estimate is given.}
The estimate treats the reviewed decisions of each band as a random
sample of that band.  That holds when every drawn decision of every
band has been reviewed, so it is given only then.  While some are not
yet reviewed, the reviewed part is a random sample of its band only if
which decisions got reviewed had nothing to do with the decisions, the
same caveat as for a single sample, and here it would also weight one
band's partial review against another's; so no estimate is given, and
the sentence says how many decisions are left.  Decisions not found in
the sessions reported on also withhold the estimate: they are lost
whole sessions or papers, not a random part of a band.  Decisions reset
since the draw cannot be reviewed any more; they are left out of $n_h$
and named in the sentence.  Leaving them out is sound only if resetting
them had nothing to do with whether the machine was right, which the
sentence leaves to the reader to judge by saying how many there were.
A band whose every drawn decision was reset has nothing left to
estimate from, and withholds the estimate.  The populations $N_h$ are
those of the draw, so the estimate is for the decisions as they were
when the sample was drawn.  A band whose reviewed decisions were all confirmed contributes
zero to the estimate; its own interval in the per-band lines shows how
many changed decisions that observation leaves room for.

\paragraph{Why no interval for the whole population.}
Franco and colleagues compare intervals for exactly this kind of
estimate.  For a proportion from a stratified or clustered sample they
replace the sample size of a binomial interval by an effective sample
size, $\hat n_{\mathrm{eff}} = n / \widehat{\mathit{Deff}}$, from an
estimate of the design effect (their equations (9), p.~9, and (13) and
(14), p.~11), and \enquote{recommend intervals of the Wilson or Bayes
uniform-prior form} (abstract, p.~1).  Their results, though, are
\enquote{conditional on a positive estimated variance}: \enquote{We set
aside samples for which the estimated sampling variance is 0} (p.~3).
Their variance estimate, equation~(13), adds up within-band sample
variances, and a band in which every reviewed decision was confirmed
has a sample variance of zero.  So when every band agrees with the
machine, the estimated variance is zero and the effective sample size
is not defined, and that is the case they set aside.  The smallest
expected proportion in their simulations is 0.05, and the smallest
sample 30 (the factorial design, p.~15); the samples [[llm sample]]
draws, and the rates they find, can fall below both, where the paper
reports nothing.  (The counter-search for this choice also found a
study of intervals for proportions from stratified samples by Stehman
and Xing, \url{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2022.113193}; its full
text could not be obtained, and nothing here rests on it.)  An interval
from the effective sample size would thus rest on a recommendation that
does not cover the case of bands that all agree, nor proportions and
samples as small as these.  The report gives the point estimate, which
needs no such assumption, and the per-band intervals, which are
computed as for a single sample, and states that no interval is given
for the whole.

\paragraph{Putting the bands back together.}
[[group_sample_reports]] replaces the band reports of one group by one
[[StratifiedReport]] at the place of its first band, so that the
reports stay ordered by the time of the draw, and leaves every other
report as it is.  A report that names a group but cannot find all of
its bands (a hand-edited file) is still shown, with the bands it has,
but without an estimate.

