\section{The classifier as second screener}
\label{sec:second-screen}

A review that screens its records twice has two verdicts of each
record.  The review module keeps the second beside the decision as a
\emph{screening} (\cref{sec:screenings} there), and [[sessions
screen]] records an agent's or a human's
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/253}).  This section
lets the classifier be the second screener: [[second_screen_with_llm]],
behind [[llm classify --second-screen]], runs the model over papers
that are already decided and stores each usable answer as a screening.
It decides nothing.  [[apply_llm_decisions]] is not called, so the
decision's status, tags, source, classifier trace and human record stay
as they were, and the paper is not made pending again.

What such a run is good for is narrow, and the code claims no more.
Vembye et al.\ evaluated GPT models as second screeners beside one
human.  The workflow they propose (Psychological Methods, 2025,
accepted manuscript, Table~5, p.~34) uses the model as an independent
second screener only after a test on records of the review itself, about
ten relevant and at least 150 irrelevant ones, reaches a recall of at
least .75 and a specificity of at least .8 (steps 1 to~5), and it ends
with the reviewers investigating and solving the disagreements between
the human and the automated screening decisions (step~9).  Scholar does
not run that test: it neither builds such a test set nor computes the
recall and specificity, so a second screen recorded here has not passed
it unless the review ran it elsewhere.  Cochrane's handbook found, at the
time of writing in mid-2023, that \enquote{no sufficiently large and
valid evaluations are available} of language models for study
selection (Lefebvre et al., Cochrane Handbook, chapter~4,
section~4.6.6.2).  So a second machine screen is treated as a check
that brings up disagreements for a human to settle: nothing reads the
screening to keep, cite or count a paper, and two machines agreeing is
not taken as a decision.

\paragraph{Blind by construction.}
PRISMA 2020 asks a review to report whether its screeners worked
independently, \enquote{that is, were unaware of each other's
decisions} (Page et al., BMJ 2021, n160, item~8).  For an agent Scholar
can only record that the caller said so.  For the classifier it builds
the prompt itself, so it can make it hold, as far as the session's
records go.  The papers to classify are shown without any verdict, as
they always were, and [[classify_papers_with_llm]] takes the examples
and the tag vocabulary from the session without the screened papers and
without every other record that shares a key of the same work with one
of them: its id, a DOI of a recorded version, or its title and first
author ([[same_work_keys]], \cref{sec:llm-interaction}).  So no record
of a screened work that these keys join appears as an example, and no
tag carried only by such records is offered.  What the keys miss is not
hidden: a copy whose title differs beyond case, accents and
punctuation, or whose first author is another, and that no version
link joins, can still be an example with its verdict.  The examples are confirmed decisions only:
[[--include-llm-examples]] is refused with a second screen (in the CLI
module), because it would show the model unreviewed machine verdicts of
other papers, often the first screener's own, and the second screener
would learn from the screener it is checked against.  Each screening
records this as [[blind]] on the ground [[prompt]].

\paragraph{Which papers.}
Three selections are offered, and one must be chosen: a second screen
of every decided paper costs as much as the first screen did, so it is
not a default to fall into.  [[all]] takes every decided paper.
[[machine]] takes the unreviewed machine decisions, the classifier's
and agents' calls that no human has confirmed, which are what a check
is most needed for.  A review sample (\cref{sec:llm-sample}) takes the
papers drawn in it.  PRISMA's explanation names partial checks among
the forms of double screening: \enquote{a second reviewer checking a
sample only (for example, a random sample of screened records, or all
excluded records)} (Page et al., BMJ 2021, n160, Box~3).  A selection
made by the first screen, [[machine]] (by who decided) or a sample
drawn from kept or from discarded decisions only (by what was
decided), is chosen by it, though the prompt does not show it;
agreement measured over it holds for those papers, conditional on the
first screen, not for the session as a whole.
A pending paper
is never selected: it has no first verdict to check, and the ordinary
[[llm classify]] is there for it.  Of a sample, a paper reset to
pending since the draw ([[reset_since_draw]]) is skipped and counted,
since the decision that was drawn is gone, as [[sampled_for_review]]
skips it.  A draw in confidence bands is stored as one sample per band,
tied by a group id (\cref{sec:llm-sample-bands}): a band's id selects
that band, and the group id selects the whole draw, the two ids that
[[sessions screen --sample]] accepts.  Each screening records the id of
the sample its paper was drawn in, the band's for a banded draw, so
that a report can find the sample, and so the band, from any screening.

The selection is taken in session order, [[count]] papers per run, as
the ordinary classifier takes pending papers: one request and at most
one retry per run, so a second screen costs per paper what the first
did.  A paper this model has already screened since its last reset (a
counted screening of the model's second-screen identity,
[[screener_identity]] in the review module) is skipped unless
[[rescreen]] is given, so that repeated runs work through the selection
a batch at a time.  The snapshot of a decision the model made is no
such screening, so it never makes the model skip the paper.  A rescreen
adds a new screening, which then counts for the model in place of the
earlier one (the latest record of a screener counts), while the earlier
one stays in the list.  Copies of one paper under one id are shown to
the model once and each is given the screening, since what the model
judges is the paper's record, which they share.

