\subsection{Main Synthesis Function}

The [[generate_literature_synthesis]] function coordinates the multi-round
synthesis process:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Gather kept papers and citation keys
\item Group papers by theme (or use an unthemed synthesis if no tags exist)
\item Generate one synthesis subsection per theme
\item Generate a conclusion based on the theme summaries
\item Assemble the document: provenance, synthesis, conclusion and
  references, plus---for \LaTeX{}---the decision record as an appendix
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Theme groupings for merged themes}
When users interactively merge themes (for example, combining \enquote{security}
and \enquote{authentication} into \enquote{Security \& Auth}), the merged name
does not match any actual paper tags.
The [[theme_groupings]] parameter provides a mapping from merged theme names to
their constituent original themes, allowing the synthesis to combine papers from
all constituent themes under the merged name.
This deduplicates papers that might carry multiple tags belonging to the same
merged group.

